@title The Pinball of the Dead (USA)
@file Pinball of The Dead, The (U).gba
@game APDE
@chip EEPROM (EEPROM_V122; a 4 Kbit chip, dingbat and mgba write 512 bytes)
@frozen dingbat-bios,mgba,nba

# Logos and the intro movie, title > GAME START > NORMAL MODE > the first
# table ("Wondering"), its text box tapped away, the ball launched; START
# pauses, SELECT opens DATA SAVE, A + A saves the game into DATA1. [load]:
# CONTINUE > DATA LOAD shows "DATA1 WONDERING (NORMAL)", A + A resumes it.
# Findings (recorded 2026-10-16):
# - In the live session the second reference's intro movie ran on another
#   timeline from f~700 (another scene at f900); replayed by `run` it is a
#   frame from dingbat and mgba (intro_movie_street SLIP -1), so that was the
#   session's doing, not the game's.
# - From the table on dingbat stands alone (its zombies walk on other
#   animation frames and paths; mgba and the second reference share every
#   hash): table_play is a SLIP(+0) only, paused / data_save / saved are
#   MAJOR against both. Mechanism: when the table has loaded, the main
#   loop creates two tasks back to back (IWRAM slots 0x03001E88 and 0x03001F00, status bytes
#   +0x48 = 0x82 "new"). mgba creates them at V-count 226 and 227, 343
#   instructions apart, before the V-count IRQ at line 0 (DISPSTAT 0x0029,
#   LYC 0); dingbat creates the first at V-count 227 and the second at line
#   26, after that IRQ's handler (0x0300077A -> 0x02002454), so the task
#   runner's next pass starts a different one of the two (f2856: slot
#   0x1ED0/0x1F48 = 02/82 in dingbat, 82/02 in both references) and every
#   per-task counter after it runs a frame apart. The race is about one
#   scanline after a ~100-frame load (WAITCNT 0x4314: 3/1 waits, prefetch
#   on); no console measurement says which side of line 0 it falls.
# - The ball, camera and zombies part from there. The password on DATA
#   SAVE is the same on dingbat and the second reference (3CCC604Z8SS6H
#   in the run), mgba shows another (Q8H8X3ZPZSHGW); the
#   battery files of dingbat and mgba are byte-identical all the same, and
#   every [load] cell resumes the same table.
# - The second reference writes an 8 KB file (APDE is not in emu.py's
#   EEPROM_4K) and its [load] shows DATA1 EMPTY: it loses its own save.
[new]
wait 270
checkpoint wow_logo window=30
wait 240
checkpoint intro_movie_date window=30
wait 240
checkpoint intro_movie_street window=30
wait 1170
press START
wait 90
checkpoint main_menu window=30 compare=text
press A
wait 120
checkpoint mode_select window=30 compare=text
press A
wait 120
checkpoint stage_select window=30
press A
wait 245
checkpoint table_intro window=30
tap A times=10 every=30 hold=4
wait 120
checkpoint table_play window=30
press START
wait 60
checkpoint paused window=30
press SELECT
wait 60
checkpoint data_save window=30
press A
wait 120
press A
wait 120
checkpoint saved window=30

[load]
wait 2100
press START
wait 90
press DOWN
wait 30
checkpoint menu_continue window=30 compare=text
press A
wait 90
checkpoint data_load window=30
press A
wait 60
wait 120
press A
wait 240
wait 120
wait 120
checkpoint resumed_table window=30
wait 240
checkpoint resumed_play window=30

